                    coach_default:          `Decision committed.`,
                    coach_r1_sharp:         `<strong>Sharp proximity map.</strong> You read the gap where it matters — the custom lane and the in-store moment — named “shipping air” as the cost to attack, and framed the whole redesign as collapsing distance to demand. That is exactly Wolcott’s lens: produce and provide value <em>when and where</em> the customer needs it.`,
                    coach_r1_partial:       `<strong>Partly there.</strong> You see some of the distance, but the map isn’t crisp yet. The gap that destroys conversion is the custom/next-day moment, the cost to attack is “shipping air,” and the lens is distance-to-demand — not capacity, not price. Get all three right and the allocation in Round 2 almost writes itself.`,
                    coach_r1_scale:         `<strong>That is the capacity reflex.</strong> A second plant makes <em>more</em> value, but it still sits nine days from the buyer — you’d be producing value <em>farther</em> from demand, just more of it. Wolcott’s whole argument is that value is moving toward the moment of demand. Where does the mattress get <em>closer</em>?`,
                    coach_r1_price:         `<strong>This isn’t a price war.</strong> Matching the disruptor on price without closing the time-to-demand gap just funds the rival’s real advantage — speed and configuration at the moment of demand. Cut distance first; price follows from lower delivered cost.`,
                    coach_r1_none:          `<strong>“Nine days has always worked” is how incumbents lose.</strong> SleepCo just left over exactly this. A proximity-native rival converts more of your customers every week you deliver value nine days late. The gap is real and it is widening.`,
                    coach_r1_slow:          `<strong>Low urgency is its own decision.</strong> Two partners worth ฿448M will follow SleepCo within two quarters unless delivery drops under four days. Distance compounds against you — every slow week, the rival captures more of the moment of demand.`,
                    coach_r2_coupled:       `<strong>Localization coupled with technology — the proximity pattern.</strong> Moving finishing closer to demand <em>and</em> deploying the enablers that capture and compress it is what actually collapses time-to-demand. Now make sure the bundle clears the 36-month payback and fits inside ฿180M.`,
                    coach_r2_digitized:     `<strong>You digitized the order, not the delivery.</strong> A configuration platform and 3D scanning let customers design the perfect mattress — then it’s built in your one plant and trucked nine days. What’s the point of capturing demand precisely if value still arrives a week and a half late? Proximity needs production to move closer too.`,
                    coach_r2_thin:          `<strong>You funded a little of everything.</strong> Half-built hubs, a basic platform, a couple of fitting units — and your time-to-demand barely moves while the disruptor sits at two days. A diluted proximity bet beats nobody. With a fixed ฿180M, which one or two moves actually collapse the distance?`,
                    coach_r2_overbudget:    `<strong>That is a ฿410M dream on a ฿180M budget.</strong> Proximity that can’t be financed is a slide, not a strategy — the CFO won’t sign a payback past 36 months and the board gave you one year. Which moves clear the hurdle, and what time-to-demand can you actually afford?`,
                    coach_r2_nothing:       `<strong>You’ve barely committed capital.</strong> Keeping production central and skipping the enablers leaves value exactly where it was — nine days from the buyer. The budget exists to move the mattress closer; spend it where it collapses distance.`,
                    coach_r2_partial:       `<strong>A start, but the coupling is incomplete.</strong> Localization without technology (or the reverse) leaves part of the distance intact. Proximity couples <em>where</em> value is produced with <em>what</em> brings it nearer — and the bundle still has to clear the payback hurdle.`,
                    coach_r3_cluster:       `<strong>Smart sequencing.</strong> Going live first in the wavering partners’ region concentrates your new capacity exactly where it proves sub-4-day delivery and saves the ฿448M at risk. The first customers to feel the new model are the ones you most need delighted.`,
                    coach_r3_export:        `<strong>Low-risk, low-reward sequencing.</strong> Launching the export lane first protects you from a visible stumble — but it doesn’t prove sub-4-day delivery to the partners who are about to leave. The pilot has to win where the demand and the risk actually are.`,
                    coach_r3_all:           `<strong>You switched all 140 partners on day one.</strong> The new lines couldn’t keep up, orders shipped late, and CSAT fell below where it started — proximity earned a worse reputation than distance. Sequence the rollout so the first customers to feel the new model are delighted, not disappointed.`,
                    coach_r3_resilient:     `<strong>That is a resilient pilot.</strong> You sequenced into the region that matters, capped intake to what you could delight, and met the supplier/adoption shock by re-allocating <em>inside</em> the budget. Time-to-demand held and satisfaction rose through a transition that usually breaks both.`,
                    coach_r3_ignore:        `<strong>Pushing on through the shock is how the pilot stumbles in public.</strong> The supplier slipped and adoption ran low — absorb it unchanged and late orders and a thin platform drag time-to-demand back up in front of the partners you’re trying to keep. Re-allocate inside the budget instead.`,
                    coach_r3_overspend:     `<strong>Breaking the budget to rescue the launch undoes the discipline.</strong> A second line and a marketing blitz may smooth this weekend, but you’ve blown past ฿180M and the payback the CFO signed. Proximity under constraint means re-allocating, not overspending.`,
                    coach_r4_proximity:     `<strong>That is the right reframe.</strong> A bigger central plant adds capacity but produces value <em>farther</em> from demand — exactly the trap. Showing the board that your model moves the mattress <em>closer</em> in time and space, financed inside the window, is the argument that holds.`,
                    coach_r4_factory:       `<strong>Conceding the factory concedes the strategy.</strong> Capacity was never the problem — distance was. A second plant makes more value that still sits nine days from the buyer. You’ve just argued yourself out of the proximity redesign you built.`,
                    coach_r4_price:         `<strong>Pivoting to price hands the rival its own game.</strong> Undercutting without the time-to-demand advantage funds a race to the bottom while the disruptor keeps winning the moment of demand. Lower delivered cost should come <em>from</em> proximity, not instead of it.`,
                    coach_r4_value:         `<strong>That is the defense that lands.</strong> “Value now arrives at the moment of demand — produced closer in time and space, financed inside the payback window” is exactly Wolcott’s argument, expressed in the four KPIs the board can see.`,
                    coach_r4_tech:          `<strong>“Best technology” is not the case.</strong> Boards don’t fund gadgets; they fund a closed time-to-demand gap, lower delivered cost, higher satisfaction, and a payback inside the window. Lead with the value that arrives at the moment of demand, not the kit that delivers it.`,
                    coach_r4_winback:       `<strong>That is how you replace ฿576M.</strong> Reframing against the factory, defending in proximity terms, and walking SleepCo and the wavering partners through the pilot’s sub-4-day proof gives them a reason to co-design a localized rollout — not a discount that erodes the margin you just rebuilt.`,
                    coach_r4_discount:      `<strong>A discount buys the partner back and gives away the margin.</strong> You closed the time-to-demand gap precisely so you wouldn’t have to compete on price — leading the win-back with a discount tells SleepCo the proximity model isn’t the reason to return.`,
                    coach_r4_ignore:        `<strong>Writing SleepCo off leaves ฿576M unreplaced.</strong> The pilot’s proof is your strongest asset with the partners who waver — use it to win them back or co-design a localized rollout, rather than chasing only new demand with an unproven model.`,

