                    insight_proximity:      `Proximity achieved: you moved finishing closer to demand and deployed the enablers that capture and compress it, so value now arrives near the moment of demand — produced closer in time and space, financed inside the payback window. Time-to-demand collapsed, delivered cost fell, satisfaction rose, and the CFO could sign it.`,
                    insight_scale:          `The capacity reflex: somewhere you treated this as a make-more problem and reached for scale. A bigger central plant produces value farther from demand, just more of it — the one move Wolcott’s lens warns against. Distance, not capacity, was the thing to fix.`,
                    insight_digitized:      `Order digitized, delivery distant: you bought configuration and scanning so customers could design the perfect mattress, then left production central so it still arrived nine days late. Proximity couples capturing demand precisely with producing closer — one without the other doesn’t collapse time-to-demand.`,
                    insight_thin:           `A diluted bet: spreading the ฿180M across too many half-funded moves left every one under-resourced, so time-to-demand barely moved while the disruptor sat at two days. Under a hard constraint, focus — one or two moves that actually close the distance — beats breadth.`,
                    insight_unfinanced:     `Fast but unfinanceable: you got close to the frontier on speed, but the bundle blew the ฿180M budget or pushed payback past 36 months. Proximity that the CFO can’t sign is a slide, not a strategy — the discipline is collapsing distance <em>within</em> the capital you actually have.`,
                    insight_mixed:          `A mixed redesign: parts of the proximity model came together, but localization, technology, financing, and rollout didn’t fully reinforce each other — enough distance remains for the board to question whether the gap is really closed.`,
                    insight_payback:        `The capital didn’t clear the hurdle: either you exceeded the ฿180M budget or the blended payback ran past the CFO’s 36-month window. Every proximity move competes for fixed capital under a payback rule — ambition isn’t the constraint, financing is.`,
                    insight_csatcrash:      `Satisfaction cratered in the launch: switching too much demand to an unproven model at once meant late orders and a CSAT drop that the new model didn’t deserve. A phased rollout would have let the first customers feel the new model as a delight, not a disappointment.`,
                    insight_csat_low:       `Customer satisfaction ended below where it started: the transition cost more goodwill than the proximity gains returned. Closing the distance only counts if the customer experiences it as better, not bumpier.`,
                    insight_ttd_high:       `Time-to-demand stayed too high: the redesign never reached the sub-4-day threshold the wavering partners demanded, so the conversion and retention upside the whole exercise was chasing never fully arrived.`,
                    insight_frontier:       `You reached the competitive frontier on all four KPIs and financed it — the durable lesson is that an incumbent can win on proximity, but only by coupling localization with technology, sequencing the rollout, and respecting the capital constraint.`,

